comments[1]{created_at,author,text}:
  "2026-07-31T14:15:55.282Z","harness:claude-code","Blocking finding 2026-07-31, filed as pm-xm0id4: this tranche cannot ship as specified, and the reason is a defect rather than a scoping problem.\n\nThe acceptance criteria require that applied edges are typed blocked_by, and the target population is terminal work. clearClosedBlockerSignals in src/cli/commands/close.ts:626 deletes every blocked_by row on an item when that item is closed - unconditionally, without regard to the blocker's status. So every edge this tranche recovers lands on a closed item and is one lifecycle transition from being deleted again: a reopen followed by a reclose erases the recovered tranche silently, and the only signal is a single closed_cleared_blocked_by warning that names one blocker even when several were removed (pm-scbr0s).\n\nThe corpus already shows the outcome of writing ordering this way. Of 1,900 closed items exactly one retains a blocked_by row; the untouched reverse spelling blocks survives on 189. Any extraction that writes blocked_by onto terminal work is writing into the one field the lifecycle clears.\n\nThis does not invalidate the tranche's core argument, which remains correct and valuable: explicit prerequisite statements written in item text are recorded claims rather than inferences, and promoting them to typed edges records what the corpus already says. What it invalidates is the storage target. Either pm-xm0id4 changes the retention semantics first, in which case this tranche proceeds unchanged and should be sequenced behind it, or the recovered edges are written in a form the close path does not clear, which is a decision this tranche should not make alone because it affects the whole ordering vocabulary that pm-4020c5 owns.\n\nRecommended sequencing: hold this tranche behind pm-xm0id4 rather than reworking its extraction design, since the extraction half is sound and independently reviewable. A blocked_by edge to pm-xm0id4 has been recorded."
notes[1]{created_at,author,text}:
  "2026-07-27T12:54:37.731Z","harness:claude-code","First tranche executed 2026-07-27. Result: 10 edges applied, 20 candidates dropped, and the headline finding is that the ordering deficit is an authoring gap, not an extraction gap.\n\nMethod. Two passes over every item document in the workspace (2,131 items, descriptions, acceptance criteria, comments, notes and close reasons).\n\nPass one, single-endpoint: a precedence phrase followed by an identifier, attributing the other end to the item the sentence lives in. Twenty-one phrase forms, including blocked by, blocked on, gated on, waits on, depends on, prerequisite is, after X lands, sequence after, once X lands, requires X first, X lands first, X blocks this, blocks X, unblocks X, prerequisite for X, must land before X. Forty-two raw matches, 32 distinct ordered pairs, 6 already edged, 26 candidates.\n\nPass two, two-endpoint: both ends named inside the same sentence, with continuation handling for lists written as X blocked_by Y + Z or Y/Z. Twelve distinct asserted pairs, 5 already edged, 7 candidates.\n\nApplied, 10 edges, each one a sentence in the corpus that names its own prerequisite:\n  pm-3hps blocked_by pm-801d      (asserted four times, in two independent notes)\n  pm-55ra blocked_by pm-h3no      (asserted twice)\n  pm-55ra blocked_by pm-atfm      (asserted twice)\n  pm-55ra blocked_by pm-dfg0      (asserted twice)\n  pm-114v blocked_by pm-8t5x\n  pm-13bn blocked_by pm-7qjk\n  pm-9yfo blocked_by pm-klo8      (stated in the item's own acceptance criteria)\n  pm-xspd blocked_by pm-zuw8      (stated in the item's own acceptance criteria)\n  pm-bgcu blocked_by pm-2muu      (stated in the item's own description)\n  pm-hybj blocked_by pm-iqgj\n\nDropped, 20 candidates, in three named classes. The drop rate is 67 percent and the classes matter more than the count, because each one is a way a naive extractor silently fabricates.\n\nClass one, hub-note misattribution, 14 candidates. Roadmap and pass-summary items accumulate notes that report edges between other items. A sentence on pm-doxj reading \"it now blocks pm-e9zh\" is about pm-pcys9n, not about pm-doxj, but a single-endpoint extractor attributes it to the item it is reading and invents pm-e9zh blocked_by pm-doxj. Every candidate whose subject was pm-doxj, pm-4k6b, pm-e9yevx, pm-89qv6b or pm-o043 fell in this class. On a corpus with review-pass notes this is the dominant failure mode, and it is invisible without reading the sentence.\n\nClass two, continuation over-reach, 2 candidates. A comma-separated list is not always a continuation. In \"pm-9rgaal blocked_by pm-j82fd3, pm-t4d7nz blocked_by pm-xp1xsw\", the comma begins a new clause, and treating it as a continuation of the first prerequisite list produces pm-9rgaal blocked_by pm-t4d7nz, which is backwards.\n\nClass three, over-broad scope, 4 candidates. A sentence that gates one part of an item is not a claim about the item. \"Catalog entries (14), (15) and (17) are gated on pm-vvib acceptance\" does not make the whole relevance engine blocked by the ADR, and its wave one shipped.\n\nAdjacent extraction confirmed saturated, so it should not be re-attempted. Thirty-five \"superseded by\" assertions exist and 51 of the 54 supersedes edges are already applied; the three apparent gaps are hub-note misattribution again. Thirteen lineage assertions were found for discovered_from and none survived review: the phrases that generate them, \"sibling of\" and \"class precedent\", assert similarity rather than derivation and belong to related, which they already carry.\n\nConsequence for the parent item. Ten honest ordering edges exist in a corpus of 2,131 items spanning months. That is the whole harvest, and it is not a limitation of the extractor: the sentences simply are not there. Precedence is stated in prose roughly once per two hundred items. So the remedy for the flat ordering graph cannot be a better extractor over the existing corpus. It has to be capture at write time, which makes this tranche the end of the extraction approach and the evidence for choosing the other one."
